faqItems:
  - question: "Why does my butterfly pull feel so exhausting?"
    answer: "Because both arms pull backward together with no body rotation to help, the butterfly pull demands more raw strength and timing than freestyle or backstroke. Drills like wall pulls and power pulls with a buoy build the specific strength and feel you need without swimming a full stroke every rep."
  - question: "How wide should my arms enter the water?"
    answer: "A little wider than shoulder-width. Because butterfly has no body rotation to help you get your forearms facing backward, entering too narrow forces you to slide your arms out wider before you can start pulling effectively."
  - question: "What's the fastest way to feel an effective pull?"
    answer: "Wall pull. Face the pool wall, lay your forearms flat on the deck or in the gutter, and press straight down. Doing this for five to 10 seconds before swimming or drilling helps you feel the same pressure you want to create in the water."
  - question: "How is the butterfly pull different from freestyle?"
    answer: "Freestyle uses body rotation and one arm at a time to generate power. Butterfly pulls both arms back simultaneously with no rotation, so timing, symmetry, and total-body strength matter far more than they do in freestyle."
