        <header class="page-title">
          <h1>How to Do Butterfly Pull</h1>
          <h2>The butterfly pull is difficult because of the range of motion and power needed</h2>
        </header>

        {% include "partials/Article/ArticleAuthor.njk" %}

        {% include "partials/Article/ArticleKeyPoints.njk" %}

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <p>The butterfly pull is challenging because it demands a lot of strength, a large range of motion, great timing, and a lot of skill. You must quickly get into difficult positions and then apply a lot of force to create propulsion.</p>
          <p>Here's what you need to know to do a great butterfly pull.</p>
          <h2 id="TheKeySkillsforanEffectiveButterflyPull">The Key Skills for an Effective Butterfly Pull</h2>
          <p>An effective butterfly pull requires you to push backward forcefully against the water so you can get propelled forward. This requires you to position and move your arms properly and finish your stroke effectively. Doing so maximizes propulsion and allows for a smooth transition into your recovery. If you can do these key skills well, you'll create speed.</p>
          <h3 id="SetUptheStroke">Set Up the Stroke</h3>
          <p>A great butterfly starts with setting up your stroke, which requires you to get as much of your forearm facing backward as possible prior to pulling. Because of the lack of body rotation in butterfly, you'll have to get your arms wider than you would in freestyle &mdash; a little wider than shoulder-width apart is a good target.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm01852.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=22b3261b075f4d60b4675e941a74e807&hash=D256C41B759A0DC4E06BEA7E5A4A3B52" alt="LDM01852" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <p>From this position, bend your elbows so that they travel up and out while you press down with your hands. This rotational movement ensures that your forearms are facing backward as much as your range of motion allows. Because of the rhythm of butterfly, this motion must happen relatively quickly to be effective.</p>

          <h3 id="ApplyPressureBackward">Apply Pressure Backward</h3>
          <p>Once you're in position, accelerate your arms backward to accelerate yourself forward. The challenge is that using both arms at the same time without body rotation requires a lot of strength and skill &mdash; you have to accelerate hard while keeping your arms facing backward, all without letting your pulling position collapse.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm01860.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=30049383334244ff8675b74a58b20c37&hash=B03DD29D9F30AC7AF581E5A4BF5113EB" alt="LDM01860" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <p>Although the focus is on pulling backward, you'll have an inward motion during your pull as your arms are brought into your body. The goal is to let this happen naturally rather than force it or prevent it from happening.</p>

          <h3 id="TransitionintotheRecovery">Transition into the Recovery</h3>
          <p>Keep pushing as long as you can keep your forearms facing backward. Once you can't, transition into your recovery. This allows you to maintain your momentum into your recovery, making your recovery easier, without sacrificing much propulsion. Make sure to accelerate your hands through your finish as much as possible.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm01861.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=b111404992354a2bab8175bbc02f32fc&hash=535E4BB617C7014CB5D05CB7A89332B7" alt="LDM01861" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <hr />

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <h2 id="ImprovingYourPull">Improving Your Pull</h2>
          <p>Doing an effective butterfly requires you to do a great pull effectively. These drills make feeling and doing these skills easier. The more easily you can find and feel the positions, the more you can practice them and the more likely it is that you'll start to do them when you swim butterfly.</p>

          <h3 id="WallPull">Wall Pull</h3>
          <p>Wall pull is a simple drill that helps you feel the key skills of an effective pull. Face the side of the pool in the water and lay your arms on the deck so that your forearms are flat on the deck or in the gutter, then press straight down onto the deck through your whole arm. You can raise or lower yourself in the water to better feel effective positions at different points in the pull &mdash; the initial set-up, the central part of your pull, or the final stages. Do this drill for five or 10 seconds before doing any of the other drills here or before swimming butterfly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm01778.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=c83f329674544f54b6e2afe593a81557&hash=7930379CF3F858999791417006B148BF" alt="LDM01778" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <h3 id="PowerPullsWithBuoy">Power Pulls With Buoy</h3>
          <p>The goal of power pulls with buoy is simple: learn how to hold as much water as possible. Start in the water on your stomach with your hands fully extended. From this position, set up your stroke, apply pressure to the water, and accelerate through your pull, trying to move as far forward as possible with each pull. Take your time and work on perfecting each aspect of your pull &mdash; doing this drill with a snorkel can be useful because it eliminates the need to lift your head to breathe. This drill is particularly effective when combined with the upside down paddles drill, stroke count work, or parachutes.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm02521.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=7b98355b0a3e4716a4dce6417bbdca07&hash=FBDA5ECB60AD0B1605F4059C38CF7789" alt="LDM02521" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <hr />

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <h3 id="PowerPullDowns">Power Pull-Downs</h3>
          <p>Power pull-downs integrate the timing of your butterfly kick into your pulling rhythm while maintaining the emphasis on power, helping you learn how to hold water within the rhythm and timing of your stroke. Push off the wall underwater in streamlined position, do a butterfly kick, and immediately do a butterfly pull and a second butterfly kick, just as you would when doing a pull on the surface. After gliding for a short time, recover your arms forward, and as soon as your arms reach full extension, do another butterfly kick followed by a pull and kick. The idea is to get as far as possible with each pull using the kick rhythm you'll use in butterfly.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm02810.jpg?h=1333&w=2000&rev=65af8f9d533245d08eec8df8f1d9aa0b&hash=F3A24CBC82A49C3421EA8803B91FF45F" alt="LDM02810" width="2000" height="1333" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <h3 id="UpsideDownPaddlesAndPinchPaddles">Upside Down Paddles And Pinch Paddles</h3>
          <p>An effective butterfly pull requires you to use your whole arm to create propulsion. Upside down paddles is a simple way to encourage this skill, regardless of whether you're swimming butterfly or doing a drill. By holding your paddles upside down, you're blocking your wrist from bending, which forces you to use your forearm and hand together. Another option is to pinch the side of your paddles, which forces you to keep your wrist very stiff when applying pressure to the water &mdash; if you don't maintain stiff wrists, the paddle will move all over the place.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm03081.jpg?h=1334&w=2000&rev=aaa720d5f4f94364863f724a3e12f2a4&hash=48714AD1C791E46C53EFAE209B2180C6" alt="LDM03081" width="2000" height="1334" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

        <hr />

        <div class="html-container include-in-nav">
          <h3 id="StraightArmFreestyleWithABuoyAndSnorkel">Straight-Arm Freestyle With A Buoy And Snorkel</h3>
          <p>Many swimmers struggle with doing the butterfly pull, so the preceding drills are not a great option for them. If you're looking for more upper-body work specific to butterfly, do straight-arm freestyle with a buoy and snorkel. By using a straight-arm recovery, you're matching the butterfly recovery, and this also tends to flatten out your stroke, making it more like butterfly. Although it's not a perfect match, this drill is a great way to do a lot of quality work that develops your arms' fitness in a way that can improve your butterfly skills. Mixing in this drill with the other drills and swimming regular butterfly can help you improve your skill and fitness at the same time.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="image-rendering image-left">
          <a>
            <img src="https://www-usms-hhgdctfafngha6hr.z01.azurefd.net/-/media/usms/lightroom/ldm03196.jpg?h=1305&w=2000&rev=e90aa23430e44318a8186eb9bf17c453&hash=1A4E365D7ED7A18ED9758370F0C78093" alt="LDM03196" width="2000" height="1305" />
          </a>
          <p class="image-credit">Image: Mike Lewis/@Mike2Swim</p>
        </div>

